{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 27, :comments ["<b>These are the sons of Ezer, etc.</b> The verse alludes, in the manner of the statement of Amram, of blessed memory (Yalkut Shimoni, Proverbs, remez 2): The Torah said: Why are my children poor? The Holy One, blessed be He, answered her: ״לְהַנְחִיל אוֹהֲבַי יֵשׁ וְאוֹצְרוֹתֵיהֶם אֲמַלֵּא״ (Proverbs 8:21) — meaning that through the suffering they endure in this world He will multiply their treasure in the World to Come. And this is the meaning of His saying <b>these are the sons of Ezer</b> — namely, those who desire to store away a treasure of good in the eternal realm must, in this world, endure suffering. <b>Bilhan</b>, etc. — and the verse informs us that suffering itself produces treasures filled with every good on high through their agency. As with the sufferings of Rabbi Elazar son of Rabbi Shimon bar Yochai (Bava Metzi'a 84b), who used to call them ״come, my brothers״ and so forth. From this it follows: whoever is the man who desires supernal life and to store away treasures of good, let him despise this world and its pleasures, and accept upon himself bilhan and za'avan and akan — the Targum for צָרָה (trouble) is ״עָקָא״ — for it is to his good all his days, and to fill his treasures."], :book :genesis, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 36, :notes {0 "The Targumic gloss ״עָקָא״ is cited as the translation of צרה; the wordplay links עקן to עקא by shared consonantal root."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:46:19.525648003Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}